ter he mumbles the line  the robes don't make the monk it was a  blink in your missing moments as the  subtitles were barely on the screen for  a second  the line carries a whole lot of weight  and it's a variation of a popular  william shakespeare line  the cowl does not make the monk batroc  is essentially shading sam in this  moment  who's just officially become captain  america while looking at his new getup  the villain is telling him that just  because he puts on the uniform  that doesn't make him worthy of carrying  the mantle this is especially  interesting given that batroc has once  gone toe to toe with steve rogers  in captain america the winter soldier on  the hostage-filled shield ship  it looks like bacharach might have had a  little more respect for our first  captain america  and not so much for our newest one in  episode 6 carly distracts bucky with a  phone call in an effort to gather  hostages from the grc without his  interference  bucky who should have been smarter than  this falls for carly's trick  but the two end up having a pretty  poignant conversation despite carly not  really listening to her foe  but he gives carly a stern talking to  telling her that though she thinks her  cause might justify her  actions in the end the nightmares won't  go away we know this to be true as bucky  suffered from nightmares in episode 1  ones that he lied about to his therapist  but the line to her that follows turns  out to be one of the most personal of  missions bucky could have made  whether cardi realized it or not you're  gonna remember all the ones you killed  while this might seem like a piece of  advice shared with carly in an effort to  get her to stand down  it's actually a major call back to  captain america civil war  in the final battle between iron man  captain america and bucky  we hear a powerful variation of the line  when the vengeance fueled tony has bucky  in a headlock  do you even remember them i remember all  of them but he clearly understands the  dangerous path that carly is on  and it's serious repercussions but  unfortunately she doesn't listen  and she meets her end at the hands of  sharon while battling sam  when carly and sam are fighting one  another the latter takes a pretty decent  beating from the enhanced super soldier  even though sam has new tech and the  shield carly still seems to have the  upper hand against him  and she swiftly knocks him to the ground  more than once however  carly doesn't want to hurt sam and gives  him an opportunity to walk away  stay down  no you might have picked up on it and  kudos if you did  because this was another major callback  to captain america civil war  once bucky gets knocked down steve and  tony go one-on-one in a heartbreaking  battle  tony gets the upper hand after friday  analyzes cap's fight pattern  and he knocks steve down to the ground  stay down  final warning after rising steve  responds with his classic retort  i could do this all day this itself was  obviously a throwback to captain america  the first avenger  when a tiny steve spoke the line to a  bully in an alley who knew all these  years later would be referencing the  famous moment yet again  but in a way that's unique to sam writer  malcolm spellman could have easily had  sam say  i can do this all day back to kali we  all know the mcu isn't shy about  dropping that quote  but by giving him a different line it  gave him a separate identity from steve  something crucial to his character's  story because as the falcon and the  winter soldier has shown us in its six  episodes  we don't need another steve rogers as  captain america  we need a sam wilson check out one of  our newest videos right here plus even  more looper videos about the marvel  cinematic universe are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

